<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otranto</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otranto is a town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, the easternmost town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-otranto">Strait of Otranto</a>. An ancient Greek and Byzantine port, it was captured by an Ottoman force in August 1480, when many inhabitants were killed, including the 813 later venerated as the Martyrs of Otranto. Its recovery in 1481 ended the only Ottoman foothold in mainland Italy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otranto.jpg" alt="Otranto" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otranto_-_Seafront_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Otranto lies on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salento">Salento</a> peninsula in the province of Lecce, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-otranto">Strait of Otranto</a>, named after it, connects the Adriatic Sea with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a> and separates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>. The harbour is small and has little trade. About five kilometres to the south-east the lighthouse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faro">Faro</a> della Palascìa marks the most easterly point of the Italian mainland. The surrounding region was long known for its breed of horses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Otranto occupies the site of the Greek city of Hydrus, in Latin Hydruntum, which was of Messapian origin. Under the Roman emperors it was perhaps more important than Brundisium as a port of embarkation for the East, since the crossing to Apollonia was shorter. A Fatimid fleet sacked it on 17 August 928 and carried its inhabitants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> as slaves. It remained Byzantine until it was among the last cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> to surrender to the Norman Robert Guiscard, in 1068.</p><p>In 1480 Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> sent an Ottoman fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a>, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> on 28 July and took the city in two weeks. The invaders had the commander of the defenders executed by being sawn in half, and about 800 residents were beheaded after refusing to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. They were canonised in 2013, and Archbishop Stefano Pendinelli was also killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> held the city for 13 months and abandoned it on 11 September 1481, after the Pope called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> joined by troops of the Hungarian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>. In 1537 the corsair Barbarossa recaptured Otranto, but the Ottomans were again repulsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town's main sights include the Aragonese Castle, rebuilt in the late 15th century, and the cathedral, consecrated in 1088, with a mosaic pavement of 1166 showing the months and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> subjects. Bones and relics of the Martyrs of Otranto surround its high altar. Horace Walpole set The Castle of Otranto, generally held to be the first Gothic novel, in the town, though he had chosen it from a map for its well-sounding name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otranto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ottoman capture of <b>Otranto</b> in 1480, where many inhabitants were killed, exposed the vulnerability of the long coastline until the town was recovered in 1481.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pecheneg troops still fought for Byzantium: at <b>Otranto</b> in 1105 or 1106 against Bohemond, and for Manuel I Komnenos in southern Italy against William the Bad, some of them at the Battle of Andria in 1155.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
